Falsifies `paging::aarch64::distinct_pages_take_distinct_paths`.

The defect: eight bits of stride under a nine-bit field, so consecutive levels OVERLAP by one
bit and the four fields together reach only bits 44:12 of the address. Bits 47:45 then select
nothing. Two different pages 32 TiB apart walk to the same leaf, which is an address-space
isolation break rather than a mapping bug: one process's page and another's become the same
entry in the same table.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(a, b)`, for any page-aligned `a` and `b` that agree below bit 45
and differ above it. The harness assumes only that the four indices agree, so Kani is free to
pick exactly that pair.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** The `sv39` record narrows the field and the
`x86_64` one replaces the mask with a modulo; three defects, one default method, three ISAs'
proofs, which is what DECISIONS §19 (architectural parity) buys here.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// covers the highest translated bits; each lower level shifts down by 9. Derived from
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
-        let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
+        let shift = 8 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
         ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
     }
 
